						    <h1 class="page-title ">
        MEASURE X    </h1>

							
							
<div class="entry-content">
	<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>MEASURE X</strong><br />
<strong>LIBERTY UNION HIGH SCHOOL DISTRICT</strong></p>
<p>In order to repair and improve aging classrooms, fix deteriorating roofs, plumbing, heating, ventilation and electrical systems, remove asbestos/ lead pipes, upgrade science labs, libraries, technology and other facilities to support college/ career readiness in math, science, health, engineering, arts and skilled trades; shall Liberty Union High School District’s measure be adopted authorizing $165,000,000 in bonds at legal rates, levying $20 per $100,000 assessed value (raising $11,300,000 annually) while bonds are outstanding and all funds benefiting local schools?</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>COUNTY COUNSEL’S IMPARTIAL ANALYSIS OF</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE X</strong></p>
<p>The California Constitution provides that school districts may issue bonds for the construction, reconstruction, rehabilitation or replacement of school facilities, including the furnishing and equipping of school facilities or the acquisition or lease of real property for school facilities, with the approval of 55% of the voters voting at an election for that purpose.</p>
<p>By resolution, the Liberty Union High School District has proposed that bonds of the District be issued in an amount up to $165,000,000. The measure provides that proceeds from the sale of the bonds will be used to “repair and improve aging classrooms, fix deteriorating roofs, plumbing, heating, ventilation, and electrical systems, remove asbestos/lead pipes, upgrade science labs, libraries, technology and other facilities to support college/career readiness in math, science, health, engineering, arts and skilled trades.” The specific projects are set forth in the bond project list within the measure. The measure provides that a citizens’ oversight committee will be established to ensure that bond proceeds are properly expended and that annual performance and financial audits will be conducted. The measure further provides that bond proceeds will only be used for the purposes specified in the measure and not for any other purpose.</p>
<p>Approval of this measure authorizes levy upon taxable property in the district to repay the bonded indebtedness, both principal and interest, in each year that the bonds are outstanding. The Liberty Union High School District has prepared a Tax Rate Statement, which represents the District’s best estimates of the property tax rates required to service the bonds. The estimated highest annual tax rate required to be levied to fund the bonds is expected to be $20 per $100,000 of assessed valuation.</p>
<p>Approval of the measure does not guarantee that the proposed project or projects in the District that are the subject of the bonds under the measure will be funded beyond the local revenues generated by the measure. The proposed project or projects may assume receipt of matching state funds, which could be subject to appropriation by the Legislature or approval of a statewide bond measure.</p>
<p>Of those voting on the measure, 55% must approve the measure for it to pass.</p>
<p>A ”yes” vote is in favor of authorizing the bonded indebtedness.</p>
<p>A “no” vote is against authorizing the bonded indebtedness.</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>TAX RATE STATEMENT FOR</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE X</strong></p>
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>TAX RATE STATEMENT REGARDING PROPOSED</strong><br />
<strong>LIBERTY UNION HIGH SCHOOL DISTRICT</strong><br />
<strong>GENERAL OBLIGATION BONDS</strong></p>
<p>An election will be held in the Liberty Union High School District (the “District”) on November 3, 2026, to authorize the sale of up to $165,000,000 in bonds of the District to finance school facilities as described in the measure. If such bonds are authorized and sold, principal and interest on the bonds will be payable only from the proceeds of ad valorem tax levies made upon the taxable property in the District. The following information is provided in compliance with Sections 9400-9404 of the Elections Code of the State of California. Such information is based upon the best estimates and projections presently available from official sources, upon experience within the District, and other demonstrable factors.</p>
<p>Based upon the foregoing and projections of the District’s assessed valuation, the following information is provided:</p>
<ol>
<li>The best estimate of the average annual tax rate which would be required to be levied to fund this bond issue over the entire duration of the bond debt service, based on a projection of assessed valuations available at the time of filing of this statement, is $20 per $100,000 (2 cents per $100) of assessed value. The final fiscal year in which the tax is anticipated to be collected is 2057-58.</li>
<li>The best estimate of the highest tax rate which would be required to be levied to fund this bond issue, based on a projection of assessed valuations available at the time of filing of this statement, is $20 per $100,000 (2 cents per $100) of assessed value. It is estimated that such rate would be levied in all fiscal years in which the bonds are outstanding.</li>
<li>The best estimate of the total debt service, including the principal and interest, that would be required to be repaid if all the bonds are issued and sold is approximately $350 million.</li>
</ol>
<p>Voters should note the estimated tax rate is based on the assessed value (not market value) of taxable property on the Contra Costa County’s official tax rolls. In addition, taxpayers eligible for a property tax exemption such as the homeowner’s exemption will have a lower effective tax rate than described above. Property owners should consult their own property tax bills and tax advisors to determine their property’s assessed value and any applicable tax exemptions.</p>
<p>The attention of all voters is directed to the fact that the foregoing information is based upon projections and estimates only, which amounts are not maximum amounts and are not binding upon the District. The actual debt service, tax rates and the years in which they will apply may vary from those performed as of a certain date in time and used to provide the estimates set forth above, due to factors such as variations in the timing of bond sales, the par amount of bonds sold and market interest rates available at the time of each sale, actual assessed valuations over the term of the bonds, and other factors. The date and amount of bonds sold at any given time will be determined by the District based on the need for project funds and other considerations. The actual interest rates at which the bonds will be sold will depend on conditions in the bond market at the time of sale. Actual future assessed valuations will depend upon the amount and value of taxable property within the District as determined by the Contra Costa County Assessor in the annual assessment and the equalization process.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;">s/ Denise Rugani</span><br />
Superintendent<br />
Liberty Union High School District</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>ARGUMENT IN FAVOR OF</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE X</strong></p>
<p>Our high schools are among the best in the region. Liberty, Freedom, and Heritage High Schools provide outstanding instruction that prepares students for college, careers, and lucrative jobs. All three schools have earned recognition as California Distinguished Schools and California Gold Ribbon Schools.</p>
<p>To maintain that standard, Measure X will fund critical repairs and upgrades needed for student success. Many facilities are more than 60 years old. Aging infrastructure has caused gas leaks, water leaks, sewage backups, and more than 150 roof leaks in the past two years alone. These problems must be addressed.</p>
<p>Top Priority projects have been identified at each school, including:</p>
<ul>
<li>Removing hazardous materials such as asbestos and lead pipes from older schools</li>
<li>Upgrading campuses to meet current health, safety, and accessibility standards</li>
<li>Repairing or replacing deteriorating roofs, plumbing, gas lines, sewer lines, heating, ventilation, electrical systems, and aging portables</li>
<li>Providing modern classrooms, technology, and facilities that support instruction in math, science, engineering, and technology</li>
<li>Expanding career technical education with updated labs and equipment for programs such as health sciences, agriculture, skilled trades, law enforcement, and fire safety</li>
<li>Improving student safety through upgraded fencing, cameras, emergency communication systems, smoke detectors, fire alarms, and sprinklers</li>
</ul>
<p>Every penny from Measure X will stay in our community and cannot be used for salaries. Independent citizens’ oversight and annual audits are required to ensure funds are spent as promised.</p>
<p>Whether or not you have children in our schools, strong local schools protect our quality of life and support property values. If approved, Measure X will bring up to $35 million in state matching funds to our district that would otherwise go elsewhere, maximizing the value of local taxpayer dollars.</p>
<p>Measure X will protect our schools, improve student safety, modernize aging facilities, and secure additional state funding for our community.</p>
<p><strong>Please vote Yes on Measure X.</strong></p>
<p>Seana Fippin, Founder and CEO of Red Box Business Solutions<br />
Roy Ghiggeri, Former Liberty Union High School District Board<br />
Member; Partner/Owner of G&amp;S Farms<br />
Brian Swisher, Former Mayor, City of Brentwood; Controller, Wayne E. Swisher Cement<br />
James Hamilton, Vice President, Board of Directors, Delta Community Services<br />
Eric L. Volta, Former Superintendent, Liberty Union High School District</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>REBUTTAL TO ARGUMENT IN FAVOR OF</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE X</strong></p>
<p><strong>Vote No on Measure X.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Ten years ago, Liberty Union High School District (LUHSD) received approval to borrow $122 million with Measure U. This money ought to have been spent on the “Top Priority” projects that are now listed as problems “… that must be addressed.”</li>
<li>Why was the asbestos and lead piping not removed? Why weren’t the leaking roofs repaired, or the sewer lines replaced, or the gas lines fixed, with Measure U’s $122 million? Why wasn’t student safety improved with “…upgraded fencing, cameras, emergency communication systems, smoke detectors, fire alarms and sprinklers” using Measure U’s $122 million?</li>
<li>Instead, LUHSD built a new Aquatic Center and Café at Liberty High School. LUHSD built an Auxiliary Gym at Freedom High School, and a Culinary Arts Building at Heritage High School.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Measure X increases the property tax rate by 76% over the two existing LUHSD bonds that homeowners now pay.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Measure X would add $20 per $100,000, while property owners already now pay $10.50 for Measure A and $15.50 for Measure U, per $100,000 of assessed valuation.</li>
<li>Plus, Measure X has no senior exemption.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Flat Enrollment, Poor Timing….</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Since 2016 — over the last ten years — LUHSD has increased enrollment by only 28 additional students (0.34%), according to the district’s website.</li>
<li>Adding new facilities during today’s uncertain economic times is ill-advised. The Brentwood Union School District passed a bond resolution in July, then rescinded it a week later, because economic conditions are not promising.</li>
<li>However, LUHSD ignores the economic conditions, requiring homeowners to repay $350 million in additional principal and interest.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Vote No on Measure X.</strong></p>
<p>Contra Costa Taxpayers Association, Marc D. Joffe, President<br />
Bryan Scott, CBOC Member, Brentwood Resident<br />
Kathleen E. Griffin, 29-year Brentwood Resident<br />
Tracy M Kasamoto, Retired, Courtroom Deputy<br />
Taiwo Abugan, MBA, CPA, Member, School Bond Oversight Committee, Corporate Senior Assistant VP</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>ARGUMENT AGAINST</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE X</strong></p>
<p><strong>Vote No on Measure X because now is not the time to worsen East </strong><strong>Contra Costa affordability.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Measure X increases costs for residents by $350 million of total debt service through 2058. Liberty Union High School District (LUHSD) is still paying off existing construction/maintenance bonds of Measure U (2016, $122 million, payoff 2047) and Measure A (2001, $65.4 million, payoff 2030).</li>
<li>A $700,000 assessed value home now pays $178.50 annually for these two bonds. Measure X’s payoff, adding another $140.00 assessment, makes the total $318.50.</li>
<li>LUHSD enrollment is flat, just 0.34% higher than 2016’s. So new bond funds would be used for maintenance and nice-to-have enhancements, not increased instructional capacity.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Vote No on Measure X because the expenditures are unaccountable.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>In 2016 voters approved <span style="text-decoration: underline;">Measure U</span>, borrowing $122 million (plus interest) to include maintenance issues similar to those cited now. Measure U was supposed to fix deteriorating roofs, plumbing, HVAC, electrical wiring, etc. Yet Measure X repeats those fixes now.</li>
<li>Measure X’s project list is a catch-all, not a plan. “Repair and improve aging classrooms,” “upgrade&#8230; other facilities,” and college/career readiness “in math, science, health, engineering, arts and skilled trades” covers virtually any expenditure. Voters are being asked to approve $165 million (plus $185 million interest) without a prioritized project list.</li>
<li>Measure X’s cited costs are “based upon projections and estimates only… and are not binding upon the District.” If home values drop, bond-payback tax rates could increase.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Vote No because Measure X’s project list isn’t sufficiently specific.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>California’s Constitution requires “A list of the specific school facilities projects to be funded….” Measure X ‘s Bond Project List states “The items presented on the following list provide are (sic) the types of projects authorized…. Examples included… are not intended to limit the broader types of projects described and authorized by this measure.” Sorry, that’s not specific.</li>
</ul>
<p>Contra Costa Taxpayers Association, Marc D. Joffe, President<br />
Bryan Scott, CBOC(2) Member, Brentwood Resident<br />
Kathleen E. Griffin, 29-year Brentwood Resident<br />
Tracy M Kasamoto, Retired, Courtroom Deputy Clerk<br />
Taiwo Abugan, CPA, member CBOC, BUSD</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>REBUTTAL TO ARGUMENT AGAINST</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE X</strong></p>
<p>Measure X is not about extras. It focuses on practical, needed improvements to improve the quality of education. The specific project list includes replacement of aging classrooms at Liberty High School, modernizing science labs and the Liberty library, replacing 21 outdated portable classrooms at Freedom High School, constructing a Career Technical Education Center, and building additional classrooms and an auxiliary gymnasium at Heritage High School. It also upgrades roofs, plumbing, electrical, heating, ventilation, and campus safety systems.</p>
<p>Measure X includes strong taxpayer protections. By law, bond funds cannot be used for administrator salaries, pensions, or day-to-day operating expenses. Measure X includes independent citizen oversight, annual financial audits, and public reporting to ensure accountability and transparency.</p>
<p>Opponents suggest improvements are unnecessary because enrollment is stable. Yet school facilities must be maintained and modernized to provide quality classrooms for current and future high school students. Children in our community deserve safe classrooms, updated science labs, modern technology, and workforce training facilities to prepare them for college and high-demand careers.</p>
<p>Measure X also positions Liberty Union High School District to compete for approximately $35 million in state matching funds, bringing additional investment into our community that could otherwise go elsewhere.</p>
<p>Most importantly, Measure X contains specific projects for Liberty, Freedom, and Heritage High Schools that address student safety, aging facilities, and educational opportunities. It represents a responsible investment in our schools, our students, and our community’s future.</p>
<p><strong>Vote Yes on Measure X.</strong></p>
<p>Seana Fippin, Founder and CEO of Red Box Business Solutions<br />
Roy Ghiggeri, Former Liberty Union High School District Board Member; Partner/Owner of G&amp;S Farms<br />
Brian Swisher, Former Mayor, City of Brentwood; Controller, Wayne E. Swisher Cement<br />
James Hamilton, Vice President, Board of Directors, Delta Community Services<br />
Eric L. Volta, Former Superintendent, Liberty Union High School District</p>
<hr />
<p style="text-align: center;"><strong>FULL TEXT OF</strong><br />
<strong>MEASURE X</strong></p>
<p><strong>LIBERTY UNION HIGH SCHOOL DISTRICT MEASURE X</strong><br />
<strong>FULL TEXT OF MEASURE</strong></p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>BOND AUTHORIZATION<br />
</strong></span><br />
By approval of this measure by at least 55 percent of the registered voters voting on the measure, the Liberty Union High School District (the “District”) will be authorized to issue and sell general obligation bonds in the aggregate principal of up to $165 million at interest rates not to exceed legal limits and to provide financing for the specific types of school facilities projects listed in the Bond Project List described below, subject to all the accountability requirements imposed by law and specified below.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>ACCOUNTABILITY REQUIREMENTS<br />
</strong></span><br />
Expenditures to address specific facilities needs of the District will be in compliance with the requirements of Article XIIIA, Section 1(b)(3), of the State Constitution and the Strict Accountability in Local School Construction Bonds Act of 2000 (codified at Education Code Sections 15264 and following.)</p>
<p><strong>Evaluation of Needs.</strong> The Board of Trustees of the District (the “School Board”) has identified detailed facilities needs of the District and has determined which projects to finance from a local bond. The School Board hereby certifies that it has evaluated safety, class size reduction, enrollment growth, and information technology needs in developing the Bond Project List shown below.</p>
<p><strong>Independent Citizens’ Oversight Committee.</strong> Following approval of this measure, the School Board will appoint an Independent Citizens’ Oversight Committee, under Education Code Sections 15278 and following, to ensure bond proceeds are expended only on the types of school facilities projects listed below. No employee or official of the District and no vendor. contractor or consultant of the District shall be appointed to the Oversight Committee.</p>
<p><strong>Performance Audits.</strong> The School Board will conduct annual, independent performance audits to ensure that the bond proceeds have been expended only on the school facilities projects listed below.</p>
<p><strong>Financial Audits.</strong> The School Board will conduct annual, independent financial audits of the bond proceeds until all of those proceeds have been spent for the school facilities projects listed below.<br />
<strong>Government Code Accountability Requirements.</strong> As required by Section 53410 of the Government Code, (1) the specific purpose of the bonds is set forth in this Full Text of the Measure, (2) the proceeds from the sale of the bonds will be used only for the purposes specified in this measure, and not for any other purpose, (3) the proceeds of the bonds, when and if issued, will be deposited into a building fund to be held by the Contra Costa County Treasurer, as required by the California Education Code, and (4) the Superintendent of the District shall cause an annual report to be filed with the District Board, which report shall contain information regarding the amount of funds collected and expended, as well as the status of the projects listed in this measure, as required by Sections 53410 and 53411 of the Government Code.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>NO TEACHER OR ADMINISTRATOR SALARIES</strong></span></p>
<p>Proceeds from the sale of bonds authorized by this measure shall be used only for the purposes specified in Article XIII A, Section 1(b)(3), those being for the construction, reconstruction, rehabilitation, or replacement of school facilities, including the furnishing and equipping of school facilities, and the acquisition or lease of school facilities, and not for any other purpose, including teacher and administrator salaries and other school operating expenses.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>STATE MATCHING FUNDS<br />
</strong></span><br />
The following statement is included in this measure pursuant to Education Code Section 15122.5: Approval of this measure does not guarantee that each of the proposed projects that are the subject of bonds under this measure will be funded beyond the local revenues generated by this measure. The District’s proposal for the project or projects described below may assume the receipt of additional funds including State matching funds, which, if available, could be subject to appropriation by the Legislature or approval of a statewide bond measure.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>INFORMATION ABOUT ESTIMATES AND PROJECTIONS INCLUDED IN BALLOT<br />
</strong></span><br />
Voters are informed that any estimates or projections in this bond measure and related ballot materials, including relating to estimated rates of <em>ad valorem</em> property taxes, the duration of issued bonds and related levies and collections of <em>ad valorem</em> property taxes are provided as informational only. Such amounts are estimates and are not maximum amounts or limitations on the terms of the bonds, the tax rate or the duration of the levy supporting repayment of issued bonds. Such estimates depend on numerous variables which are subject to variation and change over the term of the District’s overall facilities and bond financing plan, including but not limited to the amount of bonds issued and outstanding at any one time, the interest rates applicable to issued bonds, market conditions at the time of sale of the bonds, when bonds mature, timing of project needs and changes in assessed valuations in the District. As such, while such estimates and approximations are provided based on information currently available to the District and its current expectations, such estimates and approximations are not limitations and are not binding upon the District. In addition, the abbreviated and condensed statement of the bond measure presented to voters on the ballot label which is subject to a word count limitation imposed by State law does not limit the scope and complete meaning of the measure provided in this Full Text of Bond Measure and related ballot materials.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>BOND PROJECT LIST</strong></span></p>
<p><strong>Specific School Facility Project List.</strong> The items presented on the following list provide are the types of projects authorized to be financed with voter-approved bond proceeds. Examples included on this list are not intended to limit the broader types of projects described and authorized by this measure. The specific types of projects authorized are:</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>FREEDOM HIGH SCHOOL</strong></span><strong>,</strong> built in the mid 1990’s to serve the growing student population in the eastern part of Contra Costa County. Specific projects include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Removal of portables and construction of permanent classrooms including modern science labs and career tech educational facilities for careers in health science, engineering, law enforcement, fire, agriculture, and trades.</li>
<li>Replace and/or repair aging roofs, plumbing, gas lines, and heating and air conditioning systems as needed.</li>
<li>Upgrade classrooms and restrooms to meet current health and safety codes including for accessibility.</li>
<li>Remove asbestos and other hazardous materials.</li>
</ul>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>HERITAGE HIGH SCHOOL</strong></span>, built in the early 2000s serving the community of Brentwood. Specific projects include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Construct gymnasium and all related facilities.</li>
<li>Construct classrooms.</li>
<li>Replace and/or repair aging roofs and heating and air conditioning systems as needed.</li>
</ul>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>LIBERTY HIGH SCHOOL</strong></span>, originally built in 1902, rebuilt after a fire in the 1960’s. Specific projects include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Replace/modernize, reconstruct/expand classrooms with modern classrooms and science labs.</li>
<li>Renovate and modernize library and bathroom facilities.</li>
<li>Upgrade performing arts center and classrooms.</li>
<li>Remove asbestos and other hazardous materials.</li>
</ul>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>INDEPENDENCE HIGH AND ADULT EDUCATION CENTER</strong></span>, original building built in 1930 as an elementary school. Specific projects include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Update alarms and fire safety equipment.</li>
<li>Modernize main building.</li>
<li>Upgrade/replace aging portables.</li>
<li>Upgrade bathrooms and classrooms to meet current health and safety codes.</li>
<li>Remove asbestos and other hazardous materials.</li>
<li>Replace and/or repair aging roofs and heating and air conditioning systems as needed.</li>
</ul>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;"><strong>ALL DISTRICT SCHOOL AND OTHER FACILITIES</strong></span>: Improvements to facilities and all District sites to ensure the District provides a safe, modern, efficient educational environment and complete modern educational programs. Specific projects include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Improvements to ensure compliance with all legal codes, regulations and requirements including for accessibility and ADA compliance/barrier free access.</li>
<li>Campus security and life safety improvements including alarms, communication systems, cameras, surveillance, secure points of entry, lighting.</li>
<li>Health improvements including no asbestos or lead pipes, hazardous materials or chemicals, safe drinking water.</li>
<li>Provide modern facilities, classrooms, labs, shops and outdoor facilities supporting District programs including for math, science, health, engineering, arts and skilled trades, Career Technical Education (CTE) and other specialized programs to ensure career and college readiness.</li>
<li>Improvements for modern technology to provide a modern teaching and learning environment including related needed infrastructure and networks.</li>
<li>Ensure comfortable learning environments with replaced/repaired/upgraded HVAC systems.</li>
<li>Improvements and upgrades to infrastructure serving sites such as water, sewer, electric, gas and other utilities including for efficiency and cost-effectiveness.</li>
<li>Upgrade building envelopes, foundations, structural, and modernize interiors such as flooring, doors, windows, casework, lighting, painting, to create a 21st Century environment conducive to learning.</li>
<li>Improvements to outdoor areas to provide shade, seating, landscaping, irrigation, signage, lighting, greening hard spaces.</li>
<li>Improvements to drop-off areas, address traffic flow and safety, including paving, signage, lighting, walkways, handrails.</li>
<li>Improvements to gyms, tracks, fields, courts, stadiums, studios, spectator accommodations such as seating, food service, locker rooms, bathrooms, turf and other surfaces, and all related facilities and improvements including lighting.</li>
<li>Improvements to facilities to ensure healthy meals.</li>
<li>Improvements to physical education and athletic program facilities.</li>
<li>Provide parking related to facilities including resurfacing and/or expanding.</li>
<li>Purchase of interests in real property including parcels, buildings, rights of ways. easements and other real property interests to facilitate the projects authorized herein.</li>
<li>Address any discoveries made during planning and construction.</li>
</ul>
<p><em><strong>The order in which projects are listed in the foregoing Bond Project List does not suggest an order of priority. Project prioritization is vested in and will be determined by the District Board.<br />
</strong></em><br />
<span style="text-decoration: underline;">Furnishing and Equipping; Incidental Expenses</span>. Each of the bond projects described in this Bond Project List include the costs of furnishing and equipping such facilities, and all costs which are incidental but directly related to the types of projects described above.</p>
<p>Examples of incidental costs include, but are not limited to: costs of design, engineering, architect, legal and other professional services, facilities assessments, inspections, site preparation, utilities, landscaping, construction management by outside or on staff consultants/personnel, general contractors, and other planning and permitting, environmental review, rezoning and municipal license fees if any, legal, accounting and similar costs; independent annual financial and performance audits; a customary construction contingency; demolition and disposal of existing structures; the costs of interim housing and storage during construction including relocation and construction costs incurred relating to interim facilities; rental or construction of storage facilities and other space on an interim basis for materials and other equipment and furnishings displaced during construction; costs of relocating facilities and equipment as needed in connection with the projects; interim classrooms and facilities for students, administrators, and school functions, including modular facilities; all federal, state and locally-mandated safety upgrades; the costs of new or expanded infrastructure; the cost of providing parking and other facilities to accommodate new or expanded facilities; addressing any unforeseen conditions revealed by construction/modernization and other necessary improvements required to comply with existing building codes, including the Field Act; complying with all access requirements of the Americans with Disabilities Act and similar laws and regulations; costs of the election including costs arising directly from the bond measure; litigation costs; project construction oversight, management and administration during the duration of such projects, including by District personnel as permitted by law, and bond issuance costs.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;">Scope of Projects</span>. Pursuant to State law, bond proceeds will be expended on the construction, modernization, reconstruction, rehabilitation, replacement, or otherwise improve school facilities of<br />
the District, including furnishing and equipping, and the acquisition or lease of real property for school facilities, and not for any other purpose, including teacher and administrator salaries or other school operating expenses, in compliance with California Constitution Article XIIIA, Section 1(b)(3).</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;">Location of Projects</span>. This measure authorizes bond projects to be undertaken at all current and future District sites, properties, facilities and campuses.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;">Alternations to Scope; New Construction</span>. The scope and nature of any of the specific projects described above may be altered at the direction of the School Board as required by conditions, discoveries or other factors that may arise or become apparent prior to or during the course of planning, design and construction. In the event that a modernization or renovation project identified above is more economical or for other reasons determined by the School Board to be in the best interests of the District to be undertaken in full or in part as new construction, this bond measure authorizes said new construction including land acquisition, relocation and construction at a new or alternative site, and/or demolition and reconstruction and/or repurposing on the original site, including an expanded site, and all costs relating thereto. In addition, this measure authorizes the acquisition of real property, including parcels, buildings, necessary rights of ways or other real  property interests, required to expand District facilities, to provide access to school or other District facilities, or to provide additional school or related facilities.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;">Interim Financing Included; Joint Use Projects Authorized</span>. In addition, authorized projects include reimbursements for paid project costs and paying and/or prepaying interim or previously obtained financing for the types of projects included on the project list, such as bond anticipation notes or lease financings including certificates of participation relating to facilities projects and/or equipment previously financed. Finally, projects on this list may be undertaken and used as joint use projects with other public agencies.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;">Unforeseen Circumstances</span>. There are many factors which are unforeseen and the District cannot predict or control which may impact its ability to address some of the projects identified herein from the bond funds made available through this bond measure. These include but are not limited to rising construction costs, supply chain issues which can cause project delays, labor shortages, and unknown environmental factors or site conditions, among others. In addition, the District’s facilities needs may be greater than funds provided by this bond measure. Approval of the District’s bond measure does not guarantee that all of the identified projects within the Bond Project List will be funded beyond what can be completed with funds generated by this bond measure. As provided herein, the District Board is vested with the authority to identify project priorities in connection with this bond measure.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;">Interpretation</span>. The terms of this Bond Measure and the words used in the Bond Project List shall be interpreted broadly to effect the purpose of providing broad and clear authority for the officers and employees of the District to provide for the school facilities projects the District proposes to finance with the proceeds of the sale of bonds authorized by this proposition within the authority provided by law, including Article XIIIA, Section 1(b)(3) of the California Constitution, Education Code Section 15000 et seq. and the Strict Accountability in Local School Construction Bonds Act of 2000. Words used in the Project List such as repair, improve, upgrade, expand, modernize, renovate, and reconfigure are used to describe school facilities projects in plain English but are not intended to expand the nature of such projects beyond what is authorized by law.</p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: underline;">Severability</span>. The District Board hereby declares, and the voters by approving this Bond Measure concur, that every section and part of this bond proposition has independent value, and the District Board and the voters would have adopted each provision hereof regardless of every other provision hereof. Upon approval of this measure by the voters, should any part be found by a court of competent jurisdiction to be invalid for any reason, all remaining parts hereof shall remain in full force and effect to the fullest extent allowed by law, and to this end the provisions of this bond measure are severable.</p>
